{"kind":"artifact","text":"The gs status reference states the distinction in plain English, per ISO 24495-1, at head d52dfbfa on main 0ba86570. A section, Two kinds of staleness, says that ordinary reasoning staleness means a basis under a record was retired so the reasoning moved while the record did not, and that it blocks nothing; and that a superseded world is narrower, means the described behaviour has been replaced, and stops a merge. It then says what the default view does with each: counts ordinary staleness per lane and marks no row, keeps finished commitments out of the lists whether or not they are stale, and keeps retired state words and superseded-world notes on their own rows with their own counts. It names the escape hatches: --all prints a stale qualifier per commitment, --json is untouched, and the bounded summary keeps its per-row stale field. Both illustrative figures were re-measured on the live projection and read reported 27 (24 stale) and Artifacts 55 current, 121 stale, 1037 retired, 388 describing a superseded world. This page was not among the eleven the dot-citation repair touched, and this change adds no frontmatter line: its rests_on block is byte-for-byte identical to main 0ba86570, so the clean current anchors are preserved and every edit here is prose. This satisfies condition 3. Tests passed: go test ./internal/docset/... -count=1 uncached, all four documentation gates, and go test ./... -count=1 exit 0.","body":{"branch":"request/staleness-presentation","commit":"d52dfbfa4b33321534ba2879ea3f170b971dc45b","head":"d52dfbfa4b33321534ba2879ea3f170b971dc45b","path":"docs/reference/gs/status.md"}}